00:17:38,177 --> 00:17:42,414
for recommendation and the diversity
metrics are gonna go in different

229
00:17:42,414 --> 00:17:45,106
directions when you measure offline.

230
00:17:45,709 --> 00:17:51,377
Diversity, you're sure to see an
increase, but your accuracy metrics,

231
00:17:51,722 --> 00:17:57,232
at least offline, will go down
because your positive labels and

232
00:17:57,233 --> 00:17:59,121
your negative labels have changed.

233
00:17:59,159 --> 00:18:01,778
The label distribution itself has changed.

234
00:18:02,522 --> 00:18:06,946
Diversity of recommendations is as
important to customers as getting

235
00:18:06,946 --> 00:18:09,390
their preferences right every time.

236
00:18:10,151 --> 00:18:14,932
This will show in your online metrics
when you run an A/B experiment.

237
00:18:15,702 --> 00:18:16,631
So what do you do?

238
00:18:16,990 --> 00:18:20,921
Over the period of your A/B experiment,
you keep monitoring your offline

239
00:18:20,928 --> 00:18:25,394
metrics, make sure they stay sane,
and of course, you keep monitoring

240
00:18:25,403 --> 00:18:29,805
your online metrics, like metrics
that your business cares about.

241
00:18:29,820 --> 00:18:35,620
For example, click-through rate or
engagement rate or profitability, right?

242
00:18:35,945 --> 00:18:40,526
You want to measure all of that, say,
day over day or week over week, depending

243
00:18:40,526 --> 00:18:43,589
on the time period of your experiment.

244
00:18:44,247 --> 00:18:50,608
And you should expect short-term precision
trade-offs or accuracy trade-offs as the

245
00:18:50,612 --> 00:18:55,846
model starts to deprioritize historically
overexposed and popular items.

246
00:18:56,464 --> 00:19:03,519
So now you would end up seeing a
short-term drop of engagement in

247
00:19:03,520 --> 00:19:08,423
your experiment because you're not
showing as many popular items anymore.

248
00:19:08,875 --> 00:19:14,082
But the idea is that over the duration
of your experiment, which hopefully is

249
00:19:14,082 --> 00:19:18,484
a large enough duration where you can
measure these long-term effects, you would

250
00:19:18,484 --> 00:19:23,261
start to see engagement going up again
because of the increased diversity and

251
00:19:23,273 --> 00:19:25,758
choice that you're giving your customers

252
00:19:26,757 --> 00:19:30,475
And I briefly touched on this in the
previous slide, but I'll make this

253
00:19:30,506 --> 00:19:32,447
a bit more explicit in this slide.

254
00:19:33,565 --> 00:19:37,822
Accuracy or precision and
diversity always has a trade-off.

255
00:19:38,648 --> 00:19:44,834
So your recommendation precision is
higher online metrics on shown items

256
00:19:44,835 --> 00:19:49,348
and familiar content, and it usually
optimizes short-term engagement.

257
00:19:49,950 --> 00:19:55,519
So getting the customer's prefe-
preferences right all the time will

258
00:19:55,544 --> 00:19:58,111
give you a higher engagement usually.

259
00:19:59,357 --> 00:20:06,402
But similarly, a diversity of exposure
will also, over the long term, give you

260
00:20:06,856 --> 00:20:11,877
user satisfaction and also engagement.

261
00:20:13,138 --> 00:20:16,779
In addition to, of course, increasing
the breadth of your catalog.

262
00:20:16,779 --> 00:20:19,855
So more of your catalog is
being seen by more people.

263
00:20:20,726 --> 00:20:23,995
So this helps broader content discovery.

264
00:20:24,787 --> 00:20:26,191
And so there's always a trade-off.

265
00:20:26,229 --> 00:20:31,726
As you increase the diversity on the page,
the recommendation accuracy goes down.

266
00:20:32,889 --> 00:20:35,605
And as you increase the
recommendation accuracy on the

267
00:20:35,609 --> 00:20:38,658
page, diversity usually goes down.

268
00:20:39,351 --> 00:20:45,022
And you need to find the sweet spot here
because you don't want to show completely

269
00:20:45,353 --> 00:20:50,994
irrelevant recommendations just for the
sake of diversity, nor do you want to show

270
00:20:52,212 --> 00:20:59,127
all of the precise recommendations that
a customer would interact with, because

271
00:20:59,140 --> 00:21:03,092
that would mean they are just seeing
the same content over and over again.

272
00:21:03,222 --> 00:21:08,926
There's no diversity And in order to
do this kind of effort, this comes from

273
00:21:08,926 --> 00:21:13,634
my personal and practical experience,
of course, you have to work cross-team.

274
00:21:14,432 --> 00:21:19,322
You need support from your client
engineering and UI teams in order

275
00:21:19,323 --> 00:21:22,996
to actually log those client-side
impressions that I talked about,

276
00:21:23,342 --> 00:21:25,041
viewport tracking and all of that.

277
00:21:25,590 --> 00:21:31,812
Then you need your data engineering
teams to own that new data set, to do

278
00:21:31,815 --> 00:21:37,192
schema validation, to do data validation,
and to actually make these new signals

279
00:21:37,239 --> 00:21:39,361
available for your model training.

280
00:21:39,870 --> 00:21:43,527
And then you probably, as the machine
learning engineer in this pipeline,

281
00:21:43,540 --> 00:21:48,005
would have your own team that owns the
training strategy adjustments I talked

282
00:21:48,014 --> 00:21:52,507
about, owns the evaluation pipelines,
owns the A/B experiment design.

283
00:21:53,259 --> 00:21:56,610
And finally, you also have the
product and analytics teams where

284
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:00,173
they define the success metrics
for your online experiment.

285
00:22:00,205 --> 00:22:04,159
They align the stakeholders, and
they govern the rollout decisions.

286
00:22:04,576 --> 00:22:09,428
So you have to work across the
board here in order for a successful

287
00:22:09,460 --> 00:22:12,123
exposure bias correction experiment.

288
00:22:12,708 --> 00:22:16,585
And this was one of the
most enjoyable things of my

289
00:22:16,585 --> 00:22:18,071
project when I worked on this.

290
00:22:19,883 --> 00:22:23,114
So yeah, this brings us almost to the end.

291
00:22:23,516 --> 00:22:26,929
I'll just highlight a few
key takeaways from my talk.

292
00:22:27,843 --> 00:22:31,113
So the first takeaway was
always instrument first.

293
00:22:31,419 --> 00:22:36,821
Show that you have a problem,
then instrument the data that

294
00:22:36,823 --> 00:22:40,290
solves that problem, in this
case, client-side impressions.

295
00:22:41,144 --> 00:22:43,149
Build for experimentation.

296
00:22:43,158 --> 00:22:47,671
Make design decisions that allow
A/B experimentation down the road.

297
00:22:48,547 --> 00:22:54,506
If you deprecate your existing data set
too soon, you don't have anything which

298
00:22:54,507 --> 00:22:59,576
you can use for training your current
production model over time, and so you

299
00:22:59,577 --> 00:23:01,430
don't have experimentation unlocked.

300
00:23:01,721 --> 00:23:06,524
So you might have to take a short-term
operational loss here to maintain

301
00:23:06,532 --> 00:23:12,747
two data pipelines, but you also
have the ability to experiment, which

302
00:23:12,747 --> 00:23:14,639
you unlock because of this decision.

303
00:23:15,339 --> 00:23:18,438
Then you adjust your
training holistically.

304
00:23:18,521 --> 00:23:20,094
You update your sampling.

305
00:23:20,114 --> 00:23:23,468
You make sure you're getting enough
data for the model to train on.

306
00:23:24,002 --> 00:23:26,928
That is how the model will
be successful in production.

307
00:23:27,572 --> 00:23:31,153
And finally, experiment with the intent.

308
00:23:31,498 --> 00:23:36,032
You always track your accuracy
and diversity metrics together.

309
00:23:36,560 --> 00:23:41,950
You align your stakeholders beforehand
that they are going to see some short-term

310
00:23:41,951 --> 00:23:47,059
engagement drops so that there's not
a surprise when the experiment starts

311
00:23:47,137 --> 00:23:51,514
running, so that you don't have to
dial down your experience, for example.

312
00:23:52,380 --> 00:23:57,199
And this is very important
because you will see long-term

313
00:23:57,289 --> 00:24:01,568
engagement increases if you keep
the experiment running long enough.

314
00:24:02,671 --> 00:24:04,735
So those were all the
takeaways from my talk.

315
00:24:05,054 --> 00:24:09,363
Thank you so much for being
here, and I'll see you later.
